WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.134 --> 00:00:16.181
Matthew was sitting in the kitchen when Anne and her schoolmates burst into the room.

2
00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:19.616
They were laughing happily and did not see Matthew.

3
00:00:19.617 --> 00:00:26.529
He watched them shyly and became aware there was something different about Anne compared to the other girls.

4
00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:29.641
What was it? He could not ask Marilla.

5
00:00:29.642 --> 00:00:36.921
She would say the only difference between Anne and the other girls was that they were sometimes quiet while Anne never was.

6
00:00:36.922 --> 00:00:42.043
Finally Matthew knew the answer. Anne was not dressed like the other girls!

7
00:00:42.044 --> 00:00:48.459
The more he thought, the more he was convinced that Anne had never been dressed like the other girls.

8
00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:53.994
Marilla gave her plain, dark dresses, all in the same, unvarying style.

9
00:00:53.995 --> 00:01:02.385
All the other girls wore red and blue and pink and yellow, and Anne's sleeves did not look at all like the other girls' sleeves.

10
00:01:02.386 --> 00:01:04.974
"It must be all right," he thought.

11
00:01:04.975 --> 00:01:08.612
Marilla knew best and Marilla was bringing her up.

12
00:01:08.613 --> 00:01:15.887
But surely it would do no harm to let the child have one pretty dress—something like what Diana Barry wore.

13
00:01:15.888 --> 00:01:19.086
Matthew decided that he would get one for Anne.

14
00:01:19.087 --> 00:01:24.550
Christmas was only two weeks away, and a nice new dress would be the perfect present.

15
00:01:24.551 --> 00:01:29.032
The next evening Matthew went to Carmody to buy the dress.

16
00:01:29.033 --> 00:01:34.795
Matthew decided to go to Samuel Lawson's store instead of William Blair's store.

17
00:01:34.796 --> 00:01:38.267
The Cuthberts had always gone to William Blair's store,

18
00:01:38.268 --> 00:01:44.658
but William Blair's two daughters served customers there, and Matthew felt very shy around them.

19
00:01:44.659 --> 00:01:48.119
He would go to Lawson's, where Samuel would serve him.

20
00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:54.404
Alas! Matthew did not know that Samuel had a new female shop assistant.

21
00:01:54.405 --> 00:02:03.421
Miss Harris, the new shop assistant at Lawson's store, was dressed very smartly and wore bracelets that glittered and tinkled.

22
00:02:03.422 --> 00:02:09.168
Matthew was confused to see her, and all those bracelets made him even more nervous.

23
00:02:09.169 --> 00:02:12.524
"How can I help you, Mr. Cuthbert?" she asked.

24
00:02:12.525 --> 00:02:17.313
"Have you any . . . uh . . . garden rakes?" stammered Matthew.

25
00:02:17.314 --> 00:02:23.887
Miss Harris looked surprised. It was the middle of winter and there were no leaves to be raked!

26
00:02:23.888 --> 00:02:31.475
"I think we have one or two rakes left," she said, "but they're upstairs. I'll check."

27
00:02:31.476 --> 00:02:36.267
Miss Harris returned with a rake and asked, "Anything else, Mr. Cuthbert?"

28
00:02:36.268 --> 00:02:45.369
Matthew tried again. "Well, since you're asking, I might, um . . . buy some . . . pumpkin seeds."

29
00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:49.166
Miss Harris had heard that Matthew Cuthbert was odd.

30
00:02:49.167 --> 00:02:52.231
She now concluded that he was crazy.

31
00:02:52.232 --> 00:02:56.792
"We only keep seeds in spring," she explained.

32
00:02:56.793 --> 00:03:01.827
"Oh yes," stammered the unhappy Matthew. He paid for the rake.

33
00:03:01.828 --> 00:03:07.531
While Miss Harris was counting his change, he made one last desperate attempt.

34
00:03:07.532 --> 00:03:14.543
"Well now, if it isn't too much trouble . . . I might get some . . . I'd like to look at . . . some sugar."

35
00:03:14.544 --> 00:03:18.026
"White or brown?" Miss Harris asked.

36
00:03:18.027 --> 00:03:24.157
"Oh . . . brown," said Matthew, with beads of perspiration on his forehead.

37
00:03:24.158 --> 00:03:30.540
When Matthew got home, he hid the rake in the toolshed, but he carried the sugar in to Marilla.

38
00:03:30.541 --> 00:03:35.188
"Brown sugar? Why did you get that?" Marilla asked.

39
00:03:35.189 --> 00:03:41.257
"You know I never use it except for the porridge or fruitcake! It's not good sugar either.

40
00:03:41.258 --> 00:03:44.348
William Blair doesn't usually keep sugar like that."

41
00:03:44.349 --> 00:03:49.077
"I . . . I thought it might come in handy sometime," said Matthew.

42
00:03:49.078 --> 00:03:52.674
Matthew finally decided that a woman's help was needed.

43
00:03:52.675 --> 00:04:01.191
Marilla was out of the question because she would surely object to the idea of a pretty dress for Anne. This left Mrs. Lynde.

44
00:04:01.192 --> 00:04:06.082
Mrs. Lynde said, "You want me to pick out a dress for Anne?

45
00:04:06.083 --> 00:04:09.862
Of course I will. I'm going to Carmody tomorrow.

46
00:04:09.863 --> 00:04:16.178
Do you have anything in mind? No? Well, I'll use my own judgment then."

47
00:04:16.179 --> 00:04:20.163
"Thank you, Rachel," mumbled Matthew. He continued.

48
00:04:20.164 --> 00:04:24.992
"I'm not sure, but I'd like the sleeves to be in the new fashion."

49
00:04:24.993 --> 00:04:30.400
"Puffs? Of course. I'll choose the very latest fashion."

50
00:04:30.401 --> 00:04:38.523
When Matthew had gone, Mrs. Lynde said to herself, "Thank goodness that poor girl will get to wear something decent.

51
00:04:38.524 --> 00:04:44.314
The way Marilla dresses her is ridiculous. But Matthew noticed it!

52
00:04:44.315 --> 00:04:49.928
That man is waking up after being asleep for sixty years."

53
00:04:49.929 --> 00:04:58.111
Marilla knew that something was up, but she didn't guess what it was until Mrs. Lynde brought the dress over on Christmas Eve.

54
00:04:58.112 --> 00:05:04.207
"So this is why Matthew looked so mysterious." Marilla shook her head.

55
00:05:04.208 --> 00:05:09.341
"Anne has three good warm dresses, and anything more is just extravagant.

56
00:05:09.342 --> 00:05:12.945
There's enough material in those sleeves for another dress!

57
00:05:12.946 --> 00:05:19.759
The puffs have been getting bigger and bigger; they're as big as balloons now!"

58
00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:28.173
Christmas morning came. Just enough snow fell to cover everything in white. Anne ran downstairs, singing.

59
00:05:28.174 --> 00:05:35.922
"Merry Christmas, Marilla! Merry Christmas, Matthew! I'm so glad it's a white Christmas.

60
00:05:35.923 --> 00:05:39.677
Any other kind of Christmas doesn't seem real, does it?

61
00:05:39.678 --> 00:05:44.158
Oh, Matthew, is that for me? Oh, Matthew!"

62
00:05:44.159 --> 00:05:48.395
Matthew had sheepishly unfolded the dress and held it out.

63
00:05:48.396 --> 00:05:51.633
Anne looked at the dress in reverent silence.

64
00:05:51.634 --> 00:06:00.993
How pretty it was, with dainty frills and soft lace at the neck. But the sleeves! They were magnificent!

65
00:06:00.994 --> 00:06:07.862
"It's a Christmas present for you, Anne," said Matthew shyly. "Why, why Anne, don't you like it?"

66
00:06:07.863 --> 00:06:11.389
For Anne's eyes had suddenly filled with tears.

67
00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:21.950
"Like it? Oh Matthew! It's exquisite. Look at those sleeves! I can never thank you enough."

68
00:06:21.951 --> 00:06:29.270
"Well, let's have breakfast," interrupted Marilla. "I must say, Anne, I don't think you needed the dress.

69
00:06:29.271 --> 00:06:33.775
Since Matthew has gotten it for you, make sure you take care of it."

70
00:06:33.776 --> 00:06:40.145
After breakfast Diana came to the house. "I've got something for you," said Diana.

71
00:06:40.146 --> 00:06:47.696
"Here, in this box. Aunt Josephine sent us a big box with many things, and this is for you."

72
00:06:47.697 --> 00:06:50.185
Anne opened the box and peeked in.

73
00:06:50.186 --> 00:06:57.860
There was a card with "To dear Anne, Merry Christmas" written on it, and a dainty pair of shoes.

74
00:06:57.861 --> 00:07:00.724
"You won't have to borrow Ruby's shoes now.

75
00:07:00.725 --> 00:07:05.849
And that's good because they're two sizes too big for you," said Diana.

76
00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:09.436
"Oh, Diana," Anne exclaimed.

77
00:07:09.437 --> 00:07:29.784
"This is too much—a white Christmas at Green Gables and new shoes and a new dress with puffed sleeves. What a happy day this is!"
